{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2258 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "31fec000244b92cdc9e75bee07733c010dc2ef5b17ae12ad09e7e3bbb133ae5f" :edb/db-before-t 2257 :edb/db-after-t 2258 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535570 50 #inst "2026-10-09T02:46:41.406Z" 13194139535570 true] [17592186100557 1100 "A farmer has $500$ meters of fencing and wants to fence off a rectangular field that borders a straight river. He needs no fence along the river. Find $x+y$, where $x$ and $y$ are the dimensions of the field with the largest area that the farmer can fence off." 13194139535570 false] [17592186100557 1100 "A farmer has $500$ meters of fencing and wants to fence off a rectangular field that borders a straight river. He needs no fence along the river. Find $x+y,$ where $x$ and $y$ are the dimensions of the field with the largest area that the farmer can fence off." 13194139535570 true] [17592186100557 1145 "**Step 1**: We draw a diagram, and introduce variables for the length and width of the fencing.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067869/assets/2d81f8a2a521c0552b334dff813e37f4169d2221335ab71e391a2b0c686708ea.png)\n\n**Step 2**: We need to find the values of $x$ and $y$ such as the area\n\n$$\nA = xy\n$$\n\nis the greatest possible value.\n\n**Step 3**: This is subject to the constraint that the perimeter is $500\\,\\textrm{m}.$ This gives the equation\n\n$$\n2x+y = 500.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We make one of the variables the subject in the constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+y & =500 \\\\ y & =500-2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $y = 500-2x$ in the expression $A=xy$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =x(500-2x) \\\\ A & =500x-2{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: We solve the equation $A'(x)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\nA'(x) = 500 - 4x,\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 500-4x & =0 \\\\ 4x & =500 \\\\ x & =\\frac{500}{4} \\\\ x & =125.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 6**: We confirm that $x=125$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\nA''(x) = -4 \\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad A''(125) = -4 < 0.\n$$\n\nSince $A''(125)$ is negative, we conclude that $x=125$ is indeed a maximum of $A(x).$\n\nFinally, when $x=125$, we have \n\n$$\ny=500-2(125)=250.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the dimensions of the field with the largest area are $x=125\\,\\textrm{m}$ and $y=250\\,\\textrm{m},$ and we conclude that\n\n$$\nx+y=375\\,\\textrm{m}.\n$$" 13194139535570 true] [17592186100557 1145 "**Step 1**: We draw a diagram, and introduce variables for the length and width of the fencing.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1211/Source/Images/0726404343df680759c61d2cfbac95e0.png)\n\n**Step 2**: We need to find the values of $x$ and $y$ such as the area\n$A = xy$\nis the greatest possible value.\n\n**Step 3**: This is subject to the constraint that the perimeter is $500\\,\\textrm{m}$. This gives the equation\n\n$$\n2x+y = 500\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We make one of the variables the subject in the constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x + y &= 500 \\\\\ny &= 500 - 2x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $y = 500-2x$ in the expression $A=xy$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= x(500 - 2x) \\\\\nA &= 500x - 2x^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: We solve the equation $A'(x)=0$. Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\nA'(x) = 500 - 4x\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA^{′}(x) &= 0 \\\\\n500 - 4x &= 0 \\\\\n4x &= 500 \\\\\nx &= \\frac{500}{4} \\\\\nx &= 125\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 6**: We confirm that $x=125$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\nA''(x) = -4 \\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad A''(125) = -4 < 0\n$$\n\nSince $A''(125)$ is negative, we conclude that $x=125$ is indeed a maximum of $A(x)$.\n\nFinally, when $x=125$, we have\n\n$$\ny=500-2(125)=250\n$$\n\nTherefore, the dimensions of the field with the largest area are\n\n$$\nx=125\\,\\textrm{m}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ny=250\\,\\textrm{m}\n$$\n\nand we conclude that\n\n$$\nx+y=375\\,\\textrm{m}\n$$" 13194139535570 false] [17592186100558 1100 "Among all rectangles with area $25\\,\\textrm{m}^2$, what are the dimensions of the rectangle with the smallest perimeter?" 13194139535570 false] [17592186100558 1100 "Among all rectangles with area $25\\,\\textrm{m}^2,$ what are the dimensions of the rectangle with the smallest perimeter?" 13194139535570 true] [17592186100558 1145 "**Step 1**: Let us call $x$ and $y$ the dimensions of the rectangle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1211/Source/Images/701d1cff6dfcff15248651228352d0d2.png)\n\n**Step 2**: We need to find the *minimum* value of the perimeter\n\n$$\nP = 2(x + y)\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: The constraint is that the area is exactly $25\\,\\textrm{m}^2$. This gives the equation\n$xy = 25$.\n\n**Step 4**: Solving the above constraint equation for $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= \\frac{25}{x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into our expression for $P$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(x) &= 2(x + \\frac{25}{x}) \\\\\n&= 2x + \\frac{50}{x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: We solve the equation $P'(x)=0$. Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\nP'(x) = 2 -\\frac{50}{x^2}\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP^{′}(x) &= 0 \\\\\n2 - \\frac{50}{x^{2}} &= 0 \\\\\n2x^{2} &= 50 \\\\\nx^{2} &= 25 \\\\\nx &= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 6**: We confirm that $x=5$ is a minimum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\nP''(x) = \\dfrac{100}{x^3} \\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad P''(5) = \\dfrac{100}{5^3} = \\dfrac 4 5 > 0\\,\n$$\n\nSince $P''(5)$ is positive, we conclude that $x=5$ is indeed a local minimum of the function $P(x)$.\n\nFinally, when $x=5$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= \\frac{25}{5} = 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the dimensions of the rectangle with the smallest perimeter are\n\n$$\nx=5\\,\\textrm{m}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ny=5\\,\\textrm{m}\n$$\n\nIn other words, the rectangle with the smallest perimeter is a square with sides of length $5\\,\\textrm{m}$." 13194139535570 false] [17592186100558 1145 "**Step 1**: Let us call $x$ and $y$ the dimensions of the rectangle. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067869/assets/a80e6f5620ba4e77487b675390fa04af778e538ecedbd55c783194cf3ab5772b.png)\n\n**Step 2**: We need to find the *minimum* value of the perimeter\n\n$$\nP = 2(x + y).\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: The constraint is that the area is exactly $25\\,\\textrm{m}^2.$ This gives the equation\n\n$$\nxy = 25.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Solving the above constraint equation for $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{25}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into our expression for $P$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =2(x+\\frac{25}{x}) \\\\  & =2x+\\frac{50}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: We solve the equation $P'(x)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\nP'(x) = 2 -\\frac{50}{x^2},\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 2-\\frac{50}{{x}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2} & =50 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =25 \\\\ x & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 6**: We confirm that $x=5$ is a minimum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\nP''(x) = \\dfrac{100}{x^3} \\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad P''(5) = \\dfrac{100}{5^3} = \\dfrac 4 5 > 0\\,.\n$$\n\nSince $P''(5)$ is positive, we conclude that $x=5$ is indeed a local minimum of the function $P(x).$\n\nFinally, when $x=5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{25}{5}=5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the dimensions of the rectangle with the smallest perimeter are $x=5\\,\\textrm{m}$ and $y=5\\,\\textrm{m}.$ In other words, the rectangle with the smallest perimeter is a square with sides of length $5\\,\\textrm{m}.$" 13194139535570 true] [17592186100747 1020 #edb/ref 17592186355579 13194139535570 true] [17592186100747 1020 #edb/ref 17592186355586 13194139535570 true] [17592186100747 1020 #edb/ref 17592186355600 13194139535570 true] [17592186100747 1020 #edb/ref 17592186355607 13194139535570 true] [17592186100748 1020 #edb/ref 17592186355593 13194139535570 true] [17592186100748 1020 #edb/ref 17592186355611 13194139535570 true] [17592186100748 1020 #edb/ref 17592186355614 13194139535570 true] [17592186100748 1020 #edb/ref 17592186355618 13194139535570 true] [17592186100748 1020 #edb/ref 17592186355621 13194139535570 true] [17592186101172 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535570 true] [17592186101172 1100 "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nA builder has $320$ meters of fencing and she wants to enclose a rectangular parking lot. One side next to a building and so this side doesn't need to be fenced. Find $x + y$, where $x$ and $y$ are the dimensions of the parking lot that has the greatest area." 13194139535570 false] [17592186101172 1100 "A builder has $320$ meters of fencing and she wants to enclose a rectangular parking lot. One side next to a building and so this side doesn't need to be fenced. Find $x+y,$ where $x$ and $y$ are the dimensions of the parking lot that has the greatest area." 13194139535570 true] [17592186101172 1145 "**Step 1**: We draw a diagram, and introduce variables for the length and width of the fencing.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067869/assets/275803f7f72469a8427b137b5e8523475cc0605f033bedaa2280a5d28f453c38.png)\n\n**Step 2**: We need to find the values of $x$ and $y$ such as the area\n\n$$\nA=xy\n$$\n\nis the greatest possible value.\n\n**Step 3**: This is subject to the constraint that the perimeter is $320\\,\\text{m}.$ This gives the equation\n\n$$\nx+2y=320.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We make one of the variables the subject in the constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2y & =320 \\\\ x & =320-2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $x=320-2y$ in the expression $A=xy$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =(320-2y)y \\\\ A & =320y-2{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: We solve the equation ${A}^{′}(y)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{A}^{′}(y)=320-4y,\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{′}(y) & =0 \\\\ 320-4y & =0 \\\\ 4y & =320 \\\\ y & =\\frac{320}{4} \\\\ y & =80.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 6**: We confirm that $y=80$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{A}^{″}(y)=-4\\,⟹\\,{A}^{″}(80)=-4<0.\n$$\n\nSince ${A}^{″}(80)$ is negative, we conclude that $y=80$ is indeed a maximum of $A(y).$\n\nFinally, when $y=80$, we have \n\n$$\nx=320-2(80)=160.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the dimensions of the field with the largest area are $x=160$ and $y=80,$ and we conclude that\n\n$$\nx+y=240.\n$$" 13194139535570 true] [17592186355579 1017 #edb/ref 17592186355580 13194139535570 true] [17592186355579 1089 "q-104195" 13194139535570 true] [17592186355579 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535570 true] [17592186355579 1100 "A gardener wants to fence a rectangular flowerbed with $15$ meters of fencing. To two decimal places, what is the largest area that he can make with the fencing?" 13194139535570 true] [17592186355579 1113 #uuid "1af9295c-5eff-510e-ba44-71a6e28cc19c" 13194139535570 true] [17592186355579 1145 "**Step 1**: Let us call $x$ and $y$ the dimensions of the rectangle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067869/assets/bebb7c4ca5e38d75136146f6e4547b0e72d529ce24453a3d0212bca1ffbfe4c0.png)\n\n**Step 2**: We need to find the values of $x$ and $y$ such as the area\n\n$$\nA=xy\n$$\n\nis the greatest possible value.\n\n**Step 3**: This is subject to the constraint that the perimeter is $15.$ This gives the equation\n\n$$\n2x+2y=15.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We make one of the variables the subject in the constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+2y & =15 \\\\ y & =\\frac{15}{2}-x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $y=\\frac{15}{2}-x$ in the expression $A=xy$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =x(\\frac{15}{2}-x) \\\\ A & =\\frac{15}{2}x-{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: We solve the equation ${A}^{′}(x)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{A}^{′}(x)=\\frac{15}{2}-2x,\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{15}{2}-2x & =0 \\\\ 2x & =\\frac{15}{2} \\\\ x & =\\frac{15}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 6**: We confirm that $x=\\frac{15}{4}$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{A}^{″}(x)=-2\\,⟹\\,{A}^{″}(\\frac{15}{4})=-2<0.\n$$\n\nSince ${A}^{″}(\\frac{15}{4})$ is negative, we conclude that $x=\\frac{15}{4}$ is indeed a maximum of $A(x).$\n\nFinally, when $x=\\frac{15}{4}$, we have \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{15}{2}-\\frac{15}{4}=\\frac{15}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the dimensions of the rectangle with the largest area are $x=\\frac{15}{4}$ and $y=\\frac{15}{4},$ and we conclude that\n\n$$\nA=xy=\\frac{15}{4}\\cdot \\frac{15}{4}≈14.06\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." 13194139535570 true] [17592186355580 1000 #edb/ref 17592186355581 13194139535570 true] [17592186355580 1000 #edb/ref 17592186355582 13194139535570 true] [17592186355580 1000 #edb/ref 17592186355583 13194139535570 true] [17592186355580 1000 #edb/ref 17592186355584 13194139535570 true] [17592186355580 1000 #edb/ref 17592186355585 13194139535570 true] [17592186355580 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535570 true] [17592186355580 1041 "selection" 13194139535570 true] [17592186355580 1104 #edb/ref 17592186355584 13194139535570 true] [17592186355580 1110 #uuid "36d6f053-48df-55c3-8bcf-7e55615e18dd" 13194139535570 true] [17592186355581 1034 "12.93\\,{m}^{2}" 13194139535570 true] [17592186355581 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535570 true] [17592186355581 1111 #uuid "3f3bd584-58d2-5706-89c3-ad0d0ce8df59" 13194139535570 true] [17592186355582 1034 "13.72\\,{m}^{2}" 13194139535570 true] [17592186355582 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535570 true] [17592186355582 1111 #uuid "1de19a4b-589b-58e7-9181-670e8dca2cad" 13194139535570 true] [17592186355583 1034 "17.35\\,{m}^{2}" 13194139535570 true] [17592186355583 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535570 true] [17592186355583 1111 #uuid "0ba2c321-786f-5ff3-ad5a-1c42780e199f" 13194139535570 true] [17592186355584 1034 "14.06\\,{m}^{2}" 13194139535570 true] [17592186355584 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535570 true] [17592186355584 1111 #uuid "2c905f32-6daf-55bb-9c96-6bfed2387b02" 13194139535570 true] [17592186355585 1034 "16.08\\,{m}^{2}" 13194139535570 true] [17592186355585 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535570 true] [17592186355585 1111 #uuid "77cf91d1-2f7d-59b3-8b9c-61cc6d2abfb8" 13194139535570 true] [17592186355586 1017 #edb/ref 17592186355587 13194139535570 true] [17592186355586 1089 "q-14630" 13194139535570 true] [17592186355586 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535570 true] [17592186355586 1100 "A rectangle has a perimeter of $11$ units. What is the largest possible area of the rectangle, rounded to two decimal places?" 13194139535570 true] [17592186355586 1113 #uuid "a079b2a5-f952-5f6a-93f2-9ea342f33a53" 13194139535570 true] [17592186355586 1145 "**Step 1**: Let us call $x$ and $y$ the dimensions of the rectangle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067869/assets/bebb7c4ca5e38d75136146f6e4547b0e72d529ce24453a3d0212bca1ffbfe4c0.png)\n\n**Step 2**: We need to find the values of $x$ and $y$ such as the area\n\n$$\nA=xy\n$$\n\nis the greatest possible value.\n\n**Step 3**: This is subject to the constraint that the perimeter is $11.$ This gives the equation\n\n$$\n2x+2y=11.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We make one of the variables the subject in the constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+2y & =11 \\\\ y & =\\frac{11}{2}-x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $y=\\frac{11}{2}-x$ in the expression $A=xy$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =x(\\frac{11}{2}-x) \\\\ A & =\\frac{11}{2}x-{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: We solve the equation ${A}^{′}(x)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{A}^{′}(x)=\\frac{11}{2}-2x,\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{11}{2}-2x & =0 \\\\ 2x & =\\frac{11}{2} \\\\ x & =\\frac{11}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 6**: We confirm that $x=\\frac{11}{4}$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{A}^{″}(x)=-2\\,⟹\\,{A}^{″}(\\frac{11}{4})=-2<0.\n$$\n\nSince ${A}^{″}(\\frac{11}{4})$ is negative, we conclude that $x=\\frac{11}{4}$ is indeed a maximum of $A(x).$\n\nFinally, when $x=\\frac{11}{4}$, we have \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{11}{2}-\\frac{11}{4}=\\frac{11}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the dimensions of the rectangle with the largest area are $x=\\frac{11}{4}$ and $y=\\frac{11}{4},$ and we conclude that\n\n$$\nA=xy={(\\frac{11}{4})}^{2}≈7.56.\n$$" 13194139535570 true] [17592186355587 1000 #edb/ref 17592186355588 13194139535570 true] [17592186355587 1000 #edb/ref 17592186355589 13194139535570 true] [17592186355587 1000 #edb/ref 17592186355590 13194139535570 true] [17592186355587 1000 #edb/ref 17592186355591 13194139535570 true] [17592186355587 1000 #edb/ref 17592186355592 13194139535570 true] [17592186355587 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535570 true] [17592186355587 1041 "selection" 13194139535570 true] [17592186355587 1104 #edb/ref 17592186355590 13194139535570 true] [17592186355587 1110 #uuid "f3954a5c-703d-5f7e-a6da-7838cdb52e0e" 13194139535570 true] [17592186355588 1034 "$5.5$ square units" 13194139535570 true] [17592186355588 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535570 true] [17592186355588 1111 #uuid "bdb7058b-84d3-5da2-be21-a42a427ca31c" 13194139535570 true] [17592186355589 1034 "$1.38$ square units" 13194139535570 true] [17592186355589 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535570 true] [17592186355589 1111 #uuid "3e97a45f-03ce-533b-93b6-a3994bea7d73" 13194139535570 true] [17592186355590 1034 "$7.56$ square units" 13194139535570 true] [17592186355590 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535570 true] [17592186355590 1111 #uuid "44b15e88-92fd-5c48-bcb3-2d83cb9647df" 13194139535570 true] [17592186355591 1034 "$3.32$ square units" 13194139535570 true] [17592186355591 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535570 true] [17592186355591 1111 #uuid "de75c14a-3934-577f-9fb7-7c1abcd6cbf8" 13194139535570 true] [17592186355592 1034 "$2.75$ square units" 13194139535570 true] [17592186355592 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535570 true] [17592186355592 1111 #uuid "dec4a3d4-22f4-52ff-8bc0-ef319c83c935" 13194139535570 true] [17592186355593 1017 #edb/ref 17592186355594 13194139535570 true] [17592186355593 1089 "q-14637" 13194139535570 true] [17592186355593 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535570 true] [17592186355593 1100 "A rectangle has an area of $12$ square units. What is the minimum possible perimeter of the rectangle, rounded to two decimal places?" 13194139535570 true] [17592186355593 1113 #uuid "2039ef62-539b-53d1-9128-d53416c47d5e" 13194139535570 true] [17592186355593 1145 "**Step 1**: Let us call $x$ and $y$ the dimensions of the rectangle. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067869/assets/a80e6f5620ba4e77487b675390fa04af778e538ecedbd55c783194cf3ab5772b.png)\n\n**Step 2**: We need to find the *minimum* value of the perimeter\n\n$$\nP=2(x+y).\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: The constraint is that the area is exactly $12.$ This gives the equation\n\n$$\nxy=12.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Solving the above constraint equation for $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{12}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into our expression for $P$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =2(x+\\frac{12}{x}) \\\\  & =2x+\\frac{24}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: We solve the equation ${P}^{′}(x)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=2-\\frac{24}{{x}^{2}},\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 2-\\frac{24}{{x}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2} & =24 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =12 \\\\ x & =2\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 6**: We confirm that $x=2\\sqrt{3}$ is a minimum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{P}^{″}(x)=\\frac{48}{{x}^{3}}\\,⟹\\,{P}^{″}(2\\sqrt{3})=\\frac{48}{(2\\sqrt{3})^{3}}=\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}>0\\,.\n$$\n\nSince ${P}^{″}(2\\sqrt{3})$ is positive, we conclude that $x=2\\sqrt{3}$ is indeed a local minimum of the function $P(x).$\n\nFinally, when $x=2\\sqrt{3},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{12}{2\\sqrt{3}}=2\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the dimensions of the rectangle with the smallest perimeter are $x=2\\sqrt{3}$ and $y=2\\sqrt{3}.$ In other words, the rectangle with the smallest perimeter is a square with sides of length $2\\sqrt{3}.$\n\nFinally, the smallest possible perimeter is\n\n$$\nP=4\\cdot 2\\sqrt{3}≈13.86\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." 13194139535570 true] [17592186355594 1000 #edb/ref 17592186355595 13194139535570 true] [17592186355594 1000 #edb/ref 17592186355596 13194139535570 true] [17592186355594 1000 #edb/ref 17592186355597 13194139535570 true] [17592186355594 1000 #edb/ref 17592186355598 13194139535570 true] [17592186355594 1000 #edb/ref 17592186355599 13194139535570 true] [17592186355594 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535570 true] [17592186355594 1041 "selection" 13194139535570 true] [17592186355594 1104 #edb/ref 17592186355598 13194139535570 true] [17592186355594 1110 #uuid "e9d1cf1f-6b9a-5c21-8008-122108fb4ea8" 13194139535570 true] [17592186355595 1034 "$6.93$ units" 13194139535570 true] [17592186355595 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535570 true] [17592186355595 1111 #uuid "96b901ee-70ea-5318-8d9a-d788e5707fc3" 13194139535570 true] [17592186355596 1034 "$3.46$ units" 13194139535570 true] [17592186355596 1103 #edb/