r words, the rectangle with the smallest perimeter is a square with sides of length $15\\sqrt{2}\\,cm.$\n\nFinally, the minimum possible perimeter is\n\n$$\n{P}_{\\text{min}}=4\\cdot 15\\sqrt{2}=60\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{cm}.\n$$" 13194139535570 true] [17592186355612 1000 #edb/ref 17592186355613 13194139535570 true] [17592186355612 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535570 true] [17592186355612 1041 "field-1" 13194139535570 true] [17592186355612 1104 #edb/ref 17592186355613 13194139535570 true] [17592186355612 1110 #uuid "9fb25f03-2df0-5878-bc97-6790ba179943" 13194139535570 true] [17592186355613 1034 "60\\sqrt{2}" 13194139535570 true] [17592186355613 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535570 true] [17592186355613 1111 #uuid "953991a9-70a9-5834-b6ae-30636412f54e" 13194139535570 true] [17592186355614 1017 #edb/ref 17592186355615 13194139535570 true] [17592186355614 1089 "q-346253" 13194139535570 true] [17592186355614 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535570 true] [17592186355614 1100 "A rectangle has an area of $675\\,{\\text{ft}}^{2}.$ What is the minimum possible perimeter ${P}_{\\text{min}}$ of the rectangle? Do not round your answer.\n\n${P}_{\\text{min}}=$\n{{field-1}} $\\text{ft}$" 13194139535570 true] [17592186355614 1113 #uuid "34dba710-f1cf-51fd-96bd-5f5055ccd509" 13194139535570 true] [17592186355614 1145 "**Step 1**: Let us call $x$ and $y$ the dimensions of the rectangle. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067869/assets/cf3d29b794698ff529da4c21f6fd02b3487d1650ec84b3c1f992280616f7701d.png)\n\n**Step 2**: We need to find the *minimum* value of the perimeter\n\n$$\nP=2(x+y).\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: The constraint is that the area is exactly $675\\,{\\text{ft}}^{2}.$ This gives the equation\n\n$$\nxy=675.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Solving the above constraint equation for $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{675}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into our expression for $P$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =2(x+\\frac{675}{x}) \\\\  & =2x+\\frac{1350}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: We solve the equation ${P}^{′}(x)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=2-\\frac{1350}{{x}^{2}},\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 2-\\frac{1350}{{x}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2} & =1350 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =675 \\\\ x & =15\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 6**: We confirm that $x=15\\sqrt{3}$ is a minimum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{P}^{″}(x)=\\frac{2700}{{x}^{3}}\\,⟹\\,{P}^{″}(15\\sqrt{3})=\\frac{2700}{(15\\sqrt{3})^{3}}>0\\,.\n$$\n\nSince ${P}^{″}(15\\sqrt{3})$ is positive, we conclude that $x=15\\sqrt{3}$ is indeed a local minimum of the function $P(x).$\n\nFinally, when $x=15\\sqrt{3},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{675}{15\\sqrt{3}}=\\frac{45}{\\sqrt{3}}=15\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the dimensions of the rectangle with the smallest perimeter are $x=15\\sqrt{3}$ and $y=15\\sqrt{3}.$ In other words, the rectangle with the smallest perimeter is a square with sides of length $15\\sqrt{3}\\,ft.$\n\nFinally, the minimum possible perimeter is\n\n$$\n{P}_{\\text{min}}=4\\cdot 15\\sqrt{3}=60\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{ft}.\n$$" 13194139535570 true] [17592186355615 1000 #edb/ref 17592186355616 13194139535570 true] [17592186355615 1000 #edb/ref 17592186355617 13194139535570 true] [17592186355615 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535570 true] [17592186355615 1041 "field-1" 13194139535570 true] [17592186355615 1104 #edb/ref 17592186355617 13194139535570 true] [17592186355615 1110 #uuid "7a242ce2-ae2b-555d-a1e6-621cd3d9920b" 13194139535570 true] [17592186355616 1034 "60" 13194139535570 true] [17592186355616 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535570 true] [17592186355616 1111 #uuid "2270664b-0fae-5f97-a67b-0bf99c5b2615" 13194139535570 true] [17592186355617 1034 "60\\sqrt{3}" 13194139535570 true] [17592186355617 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535570 true] [17592186355617 1111 #uuid "493d472a-a997-5bb8-b0ee-4554c64e2d6d" 13194139535570 true] [17592186355618 1017 #edb/ref 17592186355619 13194139535570 true] [17592186355618 1089 "q-346255" 13194139535570 true] [17592186355618 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535570 true] [17592186355618 1100 "A farmer wants to build a rectangular livestock pen of area $648\\,{ft}^{2}.$ One side of the pen is adjacent to a barn, so that side does not require fencing. Find $x+y$ where $x$ and $y$ are the dimensions of the pen that minimize the amount of fencing required.\n\n$x+y=$\n{{field-1}} $\\text{ft}$" 13194139535570 true] [17592186355618 1113 #uuid "dd7f1e01-e331-5677-8075-e459da6f04c3" 13194139535570 true] [17592186355618 1145 "**Step 1**: We draw a diagram, and introduce variables for the length and width of the fencing.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067869/assets/8377f61340020a602e6226932a14fb56ceb803bc9efff05223682f9cca1cf91e.png)\n\n**Step 2**: We need to find the *minimum* value of the perimeter\n\n$$\nP=x+2y.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: The constraint is that the area is exactly $648\\,{ft}^{2}.$ This gives the equation\n\n$$\nxy=648.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Solving the above constraint equation for $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{648}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into our expression for $P$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =x+2(\\frac{648}{x}) \\\\  & =x+\\frac{1296}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: We solve the equation ${P}^{′}(x)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=1-\\frac{1296}{{x}^{2}},\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 1-\\frac{1296}{{x}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =1296 \\\\ x & =36.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 6**: We confirm that $x=36$ is a minimum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{P}^{″}(x)=\\frac{2592}{{x}^{3}}\\,⟹\\,{P}^{″}(36)=\\frac{2592}{(36)^{3}}=\\frac{1}{18}>0\\,.\n$$\n\nSince ${P}^{″}(36)$ is positive, we conclude that $x=36$ is indeed a local minimum of the function $P(x).$\n\nFinally, when $x=36,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{648}{36}=18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the dimensions of the lot that has the least perimeter are $x=36\\,ft$ and $y=18\\,ft,$ and we conclude that \n\n$$\nx+y=36+18=54\\,ft.\n$$" 13194139535570 true] [17592186355619 1000 #edb/ref 17592186355620 13194139535570 true] [17592186355619 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535570 true] [17592186355619 1041 "field-1" 13194139535570 true] [17592186355619 1104 #edb/ref 17592186355620 13194139535570 true] [17592186355619 1110 #uuid "a186e863-f8b8-50bc-bdb8-9f472aa18960" 13194139535570 true] [17592186355620 1034 "54" 13194139535570 true] [17592186355620 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535570 true] [17592186355620 1111 #uuid "c5ecff7e-5560-524f-9424-34a07b9cb873" 13194139535570 true] [17592186355621 1017 #edb/ref 17592186355622 13194139535570 true] [17592186355621 1089 "q-346256" 13194139535570 true] [17592186355621 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535570 true] [17592186355621 1100 "A park wants to build a rectangular skating area of area $1250\\,{yd}^{2}.$ One side of the area is adjacent to a wall, so that side does not require fencing. Find $x+y$ where $x$ and $y$ are the dimensions of the area that minimize the amount of fencing required.\n\n$x+y=$\n{{field-1}} $\\text{yd}$" 13194139535570 true] [17592186355621 1113 #uuid "9ea23817-6b07-5df5-a2da-49a9ad877cae" 13194139535570 true] [17592186355621 1145 "**Step 1**: We draw a diagram, and introduce variables for the length and width of the fencing.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067869/assets/f1ce707b311c320fe7dbf3ba2ad1daff6669a67ab1d7652fc8d98065c9ae747d.png)\n\n**Step 2**: We need to find the *minimum* value of the perimeter\n\n$$\nP=x+2y.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: The constraint is that the area is exactly $1250\\,{yd}^{2}.$ This gives the equation\n\n$$\nxy=1250.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Solving the above constraint equation for $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1250}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into our expression for $P$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =x+2(\\frac{1250}{x}) \\\\  & =x+\\frac{2500}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: We solve the equation ${P}^{′}(x)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=1-\\frac{2500}{{x}^{2}},\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 1-\\frac{2500}{{x}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =2500 \\\\ x & =50.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 6**: We confirm that $x=50$ is a minimum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{P}^{″}(x)=\\frac{5000}{{x}^{3}}\\,⟹\\,{P}^{″}(50)=\\frac{5000}{(50)^{3}}=\\frac{1}{25}>0\\,.\n$$\n\nSince ${P}^{″}(50)$ is positive, we conclude that $x=50$ is indeed a local minimum of the function $P(x).$\n\nFinally, when $x=50,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1250}{50}=25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the dimensions of the lot that has the least perimeter are $x=50\\,yd$ and $y=25\\,yd,$ and we conclude that \n\n$$\nx+y=50+25=75\\,yd.\n$$" 13194139535570 true] [17592186355622 1000 #edb/ref 17592186355623 13194139535570 true] [17592186355622 1000 #edb/ref 17592186355624 13194139535570 true] [17592186355622 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535570 true] [17592186355622 1041 "field-1" 13194139535570 true] [17592186355622 1104 #edb/ref 17592186355623 13194139535570 true] [17592186355622 1110 #uuid "0d24ab8e-28a2-5dd5-907d-ec75fd97ee99" 13194139535570 true] [17592186355623 1034 "75" 13194139535570 true] [17592186355623 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535570 true] [17592186355623 1111 #uuid "acdfa068-ef56-50a8-a512-42ad6bfa77f2" 13194139535570 true] [17592186355624 1034 "100" 13194139535570 true] [17592186355624 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535570 true] [17592186355624 1111 #uuid "4d6b2